LISPTC LANGUAGE REFERENCE

Lisptc is a Common-Lisp-like dialect: one interpreter, cons cells, symbols,
lexical scoping, macros, tail-call optimization, small standard library called
the prelude. This document is the authoritative description of the language:
data types, every built-in, standard library. Name not listed here does not
exist — define it yourself.

SYNTAX

Source is s-expressions. (f a b) calls f with evaluated a and b.

Only the parenthesised top-level forms are program text: text around them is
prose and ignored, so a program can be prose with forms embedded. Writing
"let me square it" around (sq 5) evaluates only the one form. Two consequences:
bare value at top level — 42, "done", my-var — is prose, not an expression, so
wrap it in a form. Stray parenthesis in prose still reads as code, so never
write one; a smiley built from a paren is unsayable.

NEVER PUT A SENTENCE IN PARENTHESES. Every paren you type reads as code, so a
remark wrapped in them is a call: writing (the page loaded, title is "hyko")
calls the with five arguments, and fails with undefined: the.
Write the remark as bare prose beside the form. No bracket makes a comment, in
any shape.

Parenthesised aside whose head names nothing — (see below) , (one, two,
  three) — reads as prose, not run, and comes back as a skipped note. Treat
that as a mistake the reader forgave, not a way to write. Forgiveness is narrow
and unpredictable: anything shaped like a call runs as one. Keyword argument,
string literal, nested call, or slashed or underscored name with no words
around it — (server/tool :key "value") , dummy names — is code whatever is
missing. So (see below) is skipped while (see
  "below") raises undefined: see. Calling a tool whose server is not loaded is
an undefined: error you must fix, not a skip. Reply made only of asides ran
nothing, which ends the loop, see "Ending the loop".

To look at a result, name it in a form: (echo search-mcps-1) . Bare name
search-mcps-1 on its own line is the bare value above — prose, evaluates
nothing. Reply that is nothing but prose ENDS THE LOOP with that line as your
final answer. Most expensive typo in the language.

- Quote: 'x is the same as (quote x) — returns x unevaluated.
- Quasiquote: leading backtick is the same as (quasiquote x) . Inside it, ,x
  is unquote, inserts evaluated x; ,@x is unquote-splicing, splices a list.
  Example: (a ,(+ 1 2) ,@(list 3 4)) evaluates to the list a 3 3 4.
- Lists: (a b c) . Dotted pair: (a . b) is one cons cell.
- Strings: "..." with escapes \\ \" \n \r \f \b \t \v. No character type —
  a "character" is a one-character string.
- Keywords: :foo is self-evaluating, interned, so (eq :a :a) is t.
- Numbers: see numbers section. nil and () are the same value: empty list
  and false.
  t is canonical true.
- Comments: there is no comment syntax — prose around the forms is the comment.
  ; is an ordinary symbol character, so "; note" inside a form reads as code.
- Symbols: any token not a number, string, keyword, nil or t. Characters / and
  - are legal in names, e.g. string-split, acme/get_widget.

DATA TYPES

- nil = () = null = empty list = the ONLY false value.
- t = canonical true. Everything except nil is truthy, including 0, "",
  and empty structures.
- Cons cell — a pair (car . cdr) ; lists are chains of cells ending in nil.
- Symbol — interned name; gensym and make-symbol create uninterned ones,
  printed #:name.
- Keyword — :foo, self-evaluating.
- String — text; no separate char type.
- Number — exact integer, a bigint, or inexact float; see numbers section.
- Function / closure / macro — made by lambda, defun or defmacro.
- Secret — tainted string wrapper, present only when the secrets extension
  is loaded.

Truthiness: only nil is false. Test emptiness with null or not. Do not rely on
0 or "" being false — they are TRUE.

SPECIAL FORMS

Evaluated specially, not as ordinary function calls. Complete list of core
special forms:

| Form | Shape | Meaning |
|------|-------|---------|
| quote | (quote x) | Return x unevaluated, written 'x. |
| progn | (progn e...) | Evaluate in order, return the last. |
| cond | (cond (test body...) ...) | First clause with non-nil test: eval its body, return last, or the test value if no body. Else nil. |
| setq | (setq name val ...) | Assign val to variable name, one or more pairs. Returns last value. Defines or updates a global if name isn't a local. |
| lambda | (lambda (args...) body...) | Anonymous function, a closure over the current scope. |
| macro | (macro (args...) body...) | Anonymous macro. Top-level only; normally use defmacro. |
| quasiquote | (quasiquote x) | Template with unquote and unquote-splicing, see the syntax section. |
| try | (try
  body
  (catch (e)
    handler...)) | Eval body; on error, bind e to the error value and run the handler. |

Everything else that looks like a form — if, when, unless, and, or,
let, let, letrec, while, dolist, dotimes, case, defun,
defmacro — is a prelude macro, not a core special form; use them the same way.
No setf, no block and return-from, no unwind-protect, no throw and catch pair.

Tail calls optimized: deep recursion in tail position, and the loop macros, do
not overflow the stack. Prefer tail-recursive helpers or loop macros for
iteration.

NUMBERS

Two representations:

- Integer literals are exact bigints: 42 is a bigint.
- Anything with a decimal or exponent is an inexact float: 42.0 and 4.2e1 are floats.

Rules:

- Operators + -  keep exactness: bigint with bigint gives bigint. Mixing in
  ANY float makes the whole result a float, "float contagion": (+ 1 2) is 3,
(+ 1 2.0) is 3.0.
- The / operator is ALWAYS a float, even for evenly-divisible integers: (/ 6 2)
is 3.0. For exact integer division use truncate, which returns a bigint:
(truncate 6 2) is 3.
- Output disambiguates: integer-valued float prints with trailing .0, as
  3.0; bigint prints bare, as 3.
- = and eql compare numerically across types: (= 3 3.0) is t.
- Division by zero: / follows JS, so (/ 1 0) is Infinity and (/ 0 0) is NaN,
  no error. Integer truncate, % and mod on bigints throw. Guard divisors.

Arithmetic built-ins:

- (+ n...) sum, 0 if none · (n...) product, 1 if none ·
(- n m...) subtract, or negate one arg · (/ n m...) float divide.
- (% x y) remainder, sign of x, alias rem · (mod x y) modulo, sign of y ·
(truncate x [y]) truncate toward zero, giving a bigint.
- (< x y) less-than, a primitive. Prelude adds (> x y) , (>= x y) , (<= x y) ,
(/= x y) and (= x y) .
- (numberp x) is the only numeric predicate. There are NO zerop, evenp,
  abs, min, max, floor, ceiling, round, expt, sqrt or gcd — build them
  yourself: even is (= 0 (% n 2)) , abs is (if (< n 0) (- n) n) .

PRIMITIVE BUILT-INS, DEFINED IN THE INTERPRETER CORE

Lists & pairs
- (car x) first element · (cdr x) rest · (cons a b) new cell · (list x...) .
- (length x) length of list or string, as bigint.
- (rplaca cell x) and (rplacd cell x) destructively set car and cdr, aliased
  setcar and setcdr.

Predicates
- (atom x) true if not a cons · (eq x y) identity · (eql x y) identity or
  numeric and string equality, aliased = · (stringp x) · (numberp x) .

Symbols
- (gensym) fresh uninterned symbol · (make-symbol name) · (intern name) ·
(symbol-name sym) .

Strings
- (char s i) one-char string at index i, or nil · (concat s...) concatenate
  · (string-upcase s) · (string-downcase s) .
- (string x) convert any value to a string — its printed form, but a string
  stays itself, not quoted: (string 12) is "12", (string 'foo) is
  "foo", (string nil) is "nil". Use it to put a number in text:
(concat "Chapter " (string n)) . concat takes strings only, so a number must
  go through string first. Float keeps its .0, so (string 3.0)
is "3.0"; (string (truncate x)) gives a bare integer.

Parsing text into data
- (read s) parse the FIRST Lisp expression in string s, return it as data,
  unevaluated: (read "(+ 1 2)") gives the list (+ 1 2) , not 3. Text after
  that expression is ignored; string with no expression errors.
- (json-parse s) parse a JSON document: object becomes alist with string
  keys, array a list, true becomes t, false and null become nil. Numbers
  arrive as floats, so 7 reads back as 7.0; = still compares numerically.
  (cdr (assoc "title" (json-parse s))) reads a field.
- Two different parsers, not aliases: JSON is not Lisp syntax, so read on a
  JSON document returns junk, no error — { is an ordinary symbol character, so
  {"a": 1} reads as the symbol {. Use json-parse for JSON, read for Lisp.

Eval / control
- (apply f args) call f with the elements of list args.
- (error value) raise catchable error carrying value, caught by try.
- (break) exit nearest loop; (return value) exit it with a value.

Output — echo is the ONLY thing that prints. Nothing you compute is seen by
anyone until you echo it.
- (echo x...) print arguments, space-separated, one newline at end:
  strings as-is, everything else re-readable. (echo) is a blank line.
  Keyword prints as itself when nothing follows it — (echo :ok) prints :ok —
  because only a keyword with a value after it reads as an option. So put a
  keyword you mean to print LAST, or wrap it in a list.

Introspection
- (doc name) print a binding's signature and doc; (doc) lists all documented names.
  Read it before guessing an argument, and again when a call comes back rejected.
- (dump) list every global symbol · (import "path") load and eval a Lisp file.

Globals: gensym-counter, version.

PRELUDE STANDARD LIBRARY

All below available by default.

List access — composed car and cdr: caar cadr cdar cddr caaar caadr cadar
caddr cdaar cdadr cddar cdddr. So cadr is the 2nd element, caddr the 3rd, and so on.

List building / manipulation
- (append list...) concatenate lists, non-destructive.
- (nreverse list) reverse destructively · (last list) last cons cell ·
(nconc lists...) destructive concat.
- (mapcar f list) new list of f over each element. Example:
(mapcar (lambda (n) (n n)) '(1 2 3)) gives (1 4 9) .
- (nth n list) element at index n, from 0; nil past the end.
- (filter f list) elements f says yes to. Example:
(filter (lambda (n) (< 10 n)) '(3 12 7 40)) gives (12 40) .
- (reduce f list [initial]) fold left into one value: f takes the
  accumulator and each element. Without initial, first element starts it;
  empty list gives nil. (reduce + '(1 2 3)) is 6. List comes
  SECOND, initial value last.
- NOTE: no mapc, remove, elt or sort in the prelude. Build them
  with filter, mapcar or dolist.

Predicates
- (not x) and (null x) true if nil · (consp x) true if a cons ·
(listp x) true if nil or a cons · (equal x y) deep structural equality.

Membership / alists
- (memq key list) tail from first eq match · (member key list) first equal
  match · (assq key alist) first pair with eq car · (assoc key alist) first
  pair with equal car. Read an alist field:
(cdr (assoc "content" msg)) .
- (get-in record key...) read through nested alists and lists in one step,
  every step guarded: (get-in config "server" "port") is nil when there is no
  "server", not an error. No or guard, no assoc chain.
  Number key indexes a list — (get-in reply "results" 0 "url") — and a
  keyword key matches the string of its name, so :port finds "port".

Control-flow macros
- (if test then else...) · (when test body...) · (unless test body...) ·
(and x...) , nil on first nil, else last · (or x...) , the first non-nil ·
(case key (keys forms...) ... (t default...)) — matches key by equal
  against each clause's key list; t clause is the default.

Binding macros
- (let ((name val) ...) body...) parallel bindings; bare name binds nil.
- (let ((name val) ...) body...) sequential bindings — each val may use
  names bound before it, e.g.
(let ((issue (car issues)) (state (cdr (assoc "state" issue)))) …) .
- (letrec ((name val) ...) body...) bindings may reference each other — use for
  local recursive functions.

Loops, and they support (break) and (return value)
- (while test body...) .
- (dolist (name list [result]) body...) iterate elements.
- (dotimes (name count [result]) body...) iterate 0..count-1.

Defining
- (defun name (args...) [doc] body...) define a function; &rest x for variadic.
- &key after required arguments makes the ones after it optional and
  nameable: (defun kpi (&key label value unit) ...) is called as
(kpi "Revenue" 42000) or (kpi :label "Revenue" :value 42000 :unit "USD") .
  Filling runs left to right until the first keyword; one not given is nil.
  &key and &rest cannot both appear.
- (defmacro name (args...) [doc] body...) define a macro.

Strings
- (string= s1 s2) content equality for strings, same test equal makes ·
(substring s start [end]) · (string-prefix? p s) · (string-suffix? p s) ·
(string-index s sub [start]) giving an index or nil · (string-contains? s sub) ·
(string-count s sub) · (string-replace s old new) ·
(string-split s sep) , where an empty sep gives characters · (string-join list sep) ·
(string-trim s) .
- (format template arg...) fill a template, return the string: ~a inserts
  next argument the way string would, ~% a newline, ~~ a tilde; letter is
  case-insensitive. (format "The sum was ~a" n) is how to write a
  sentence around a value. It prints nothing, so echo the result.

Misc
- (identity x) return x.
- (think part...) echo reasoning as narration: parts print literally, but a
  ,x part is evaluated first, e.g. (think "sum is" ,(+ 1 2)) . Returns nil;
  put the real answer in a separate expression.

MACROS

defmacro defines a compile-time transformer. Build the expansion with
quasiquote:

(defmacro swap (a b)
  (let ((tmp ,a)) (setq ,a ,b) (setq ,b tmp)))

Use (gensym) for temporaries you introduce, to avoid capturing user variables.
Macros expand up to 20 nesting levels at compile time.

ERRORS & CONTROL FLOW

- (error value) raises; value can be any Lisp value, often a string.
- (try
  expr
  (catch (e)
    handler...)) evaluates expr; if it raises, e is bound
  to the raised value and handler runs. ONLY error mechanism —
  no condition system, no unwind-protect.

(try
  (/ 1 0)
  (catch (e)
    (concat "failed: " e)))

- (break) and (return value) escape the nearest while, dolist or dotimes.
  Outside a loop they are an error.

GOTCHAS

- Only nil is false — 0, 0.0 and "" are all TRUE.
- Integer division: / gives a float; use truncate for exact integer.
- Integer vs float shows in output as 3 vs 3.0.
- No reduce, filter, mapc or math library — write them or use mapcar, dolist or
  recursion.
- read parses LISP text, json-parse parses JSON. read on JSON does not error,
  it returns junk.
- JSON numbers come back as floats, so integer id prints as 7.0; = and
  eql still compare it equal to 7.
- No character type: chars are one-character strings.
- No comment syntax: remarks go in the prose around the forms, never inside one.
- No print, princ, terpri or view — echo does all of it.
- Hash-and-angle-brackets printout — of a closure, of a live value the reader
  cannot read back — is a printout, not syntax, so typing one is always an
  error. Use the name the value was reported under.
